'' A SINGLE MEETING A 64-BIT INTEGER STAYS A SINGLE. Lowering brought both operands to
'' the WIDER MIR type, and an i64 is wider than an f32, so 'f + q' over a SINGLE and a
'' LONGINT rounded f into the integer: 296 where fbc prints 296.5, and 'f > q' compared
'' 148 with 148. Check already typed both SINGLE (fbc's '#print typeof( f + q )').
'' starshine.bas's sphere test -- a single distance against '(S.col) - 2', a LONGINT --
'' kept 53,784 of fbc's 70,992 points.
''
'' MEASURED, fbc-modern 1.20 running the source, and tiko's build prints the same:
''     single:-1-1-1-1-1-1-1-1
''     sum: 296.5 296.5 21978
''     sphere: 70992
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/single-vs-longint.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-NOT: nearbyint
'' GOLDEN: expected/single-vs-longint.out
